  "hints": [
    "There are n - k + 1 windows. Storing each window as a string costs O(k) per window and O(nk) overall. Can each window get a compact fingerprint?",
    "Use a polynomial hash with prefix hashes so the fingerprint of every window is available in O(1).",
    "Insert the fingerprints in a set and report its size. Use two independent moduli with random bases; a hash taken modulo 2^64 gives wrong answers on Thue-Morse strings."
  ],
  "editorial": "Compute prefix hashes once. The hash of window `[i, i+k)` is `P[i+k] - P[i] * B^k` modulo `M`. Put the (double-)hash of every window into a set; its size is the answer, correct unless two different windows collide, which has negligible probability with two random-base moduli of about `10^9` each.\n\nTotal expected `O(n)`. An exact `O(n)` alternative uses a suffix automaton; sorting windows by suffix array also works.\n\nWhy not rely on natural overflow (`mod 2^64`)? On the Thue-Morse string the length-`2^j` block and its complement are different strings with *equal* hash for every odd base once `j >= 10`, so the count comes out too small. These tests include such a string.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
